---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { site } from "../site";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy: what Suijin and suijin.app collect"
  description="Suijin has no account and no server that sees your code. This page lists the few connections the app makes and what this website keeps: update checks counted per day and app version, and nothing about you."
  path="/privacy"
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>Privacy</h1>
      <p>
        The short version: your code and your conversations with agents stay on your Mac, and neither the app nor this
        website tracks you.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <p>
      This page is a summary. The full policy ships inside the app, in Settings › About, and the app's version of it is
      the authoritative one.
    </p>

    <h2>The app</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>There is no Suijin account, and Suijin sends no telemetry or crash reports.</li>
      <li>
        Your repositories, the agents' conversations and your settings are stored on your Mac, under
        <code>~/Library/Application Support/Suijin</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        The agents and tools you run (Claude Code, Codex, git, the GitHub CLI, your shell) talk to their own services
        with your own logins, as they do in any terminal. Their providers' terms and policies apply to that.
      </li>
      <li>Suijin never changes your own logins in <code>~/.claude</code> or <code>~/.codex</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Connections the app makes itself</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Updates.</strong> About once a day, and when you choose Check for Updates, the app asks
        <code>suijin.app</code> whether a newer version exists. The request carries the app's name and version. An
        update is downloaded from Suijin's releases on GitHub. You can turn the automatic check off in Settings ›
        General.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Usage limits.</strong> To show how much of a plan is used, the app asks Anthropic or OpenAI with that
        login's own token.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>GitHub.</strong> If the GitHub CLI is installed, the app uses it with your own sign-in to show and
        create pull requests you ask for.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What suijin.app keeps</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Update checks are counted per day and app version, to estimate how many copies are in use. Nothing else is kept
        about them: no address and no identifier.
      </li>
      <li>This website sets no cookies, loads nothing from third parties and runs no analytics.</li>
      <li>
        The site is served by Cloudflare, which processes requests, including IP addresses, to deliver pages and
        protect the service.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      Open an issue in the <a href={site.repository + "/issues"}>repository on GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
